09:58:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Eyes need periodic closing” is a reasonable shorthand, but biologically the periodic closing is not usually the end goal by itself. If the purpose were simply to block vision or rest the eye, a very brief blink would be a weak solution: it lasts only about a tenth of a second and barely changes the workload of focusing or visual processing. Yet people blink even when they are not visually tired, including in darkness. What makes the closing useful is what the lids do during it: they refresh the tear film, reduce evaporation, and sweep away irritants. That is why reduced blinking—such as during intense screen focus—commonly causes dry, uncomfortable eyes. So the simple version is: **We blink because the surface of the eye needs regular maintenance; closing the lids is the mechanism that performs it.** Short eye closure can also feel restful, but that is an additional benefit rather than the main driver of ordinary blinking.
